WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:07.140 align:start position:0%
So, I grew up in a place
called McLean, Virginia,
right outside of D.C.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.176 align:start position:0%
But I, like, split
my childhood, basically.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.478
So we'll start with where
I first grew up,

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:15.481
so I'm really excited
to show you guys this.

00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:18.818 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So this is my hometown.
- Wow, that's cool.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.420
<i> We were the only
 black family on the block.</i>

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.088
<i> I was the only black kid
 in my grade.</i>

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.123
<i> One of three
 black families in my school.</i>

00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:27.260
<i> And if we look, folks are in
 the 99th percentile</i>

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.362
<i> who grew up here when
 it comes to average income.</i>

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.330
<i> Less than 1%
 are incarcerated.</i>

00:00:31.363 --> 00:00:34.367
<i> We can look at teenage
 birth rate-- less than 1%.</i>

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.139
Around high school,
my folks got divorced
and we moved into the city.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:42.542
<i> It's the exact opposite.</i>

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.511
It wasn't till I got to D.C.
that I actually had a benchmark

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:47.513
for understanding everyone
didn't grow up the way I did.

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:50.216 align:start position:0%
And it took, you know,
my parents getting divorced

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:52.318 align:start position:0%
and us moving to the city
for me to actually

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:55.321 align:start position:0%
understand how fortunate
I really was.

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:59.759
Zip code influences so much
that we have got to make sure

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:02.528 align:start position:0%
that those
neighborhood conditions

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.564 align:start position:0%
aren't setting people up
for failure.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:08.634 align:start position:0%
We are keeping people
of reasonable means
out of neighborhoods.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.337 align:start position:0%
While I was doing some research,
I came across this,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.673
which is the Opportunity Atlas.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:16.709
And it's a map that shows you
the average life outcomes

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:20.546
for people who grew up
in a given census tract.

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:23.850 align:start position:0%
It's just crazy to think
that I may have only

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.586
become as successful as I am

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.522
because I spent the majority
of my childhood here

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:32.291
instead of in D.C.

00:01:32.324 --> 00:01:34.160 size:88% position:44%
But why don't we take a look
at where you guys grew up?

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.295 align:start size:44% position:56%
Dun, dun, dun.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.103 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:52.311 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Lee: So, you're,
  like in the Bay area.
- Christophe: Oh, yeah.

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:55.248 align:start size:91% position:9%
- I must have grew up in
  Cupertino, California. 
- Lee: Okay.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:58.217
Christophe: Majority Asian.
Definitely fairly wealthy.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.621 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- A lot of, like, tech,
  Silicon Valley employees.
- Lee: Right.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:05.558 line:0%
All right. So I'm gonna put in
my zip code where I grew up.

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:10.696 align:start size:72% position:28%
So I just took you guys
to Brooklyn, New York,

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.266
Crown Heights,
and immediately traveling here,

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:14.734
it's just completely red.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.603
Yeah, so, $30k, that makes
a lot of sense to me.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:20.606 align:start position:0%
But is also just--
this shows how segregated

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:22.708
Brooklyn is
and was at the time.

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:24.777
Fabiola: Yeah. So right here
at Grand Army Plaza,

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.812
it just looks so different
from the blocks

00:02:26.845 --> 00:02:28.314
where I grew up in Brooklyn.

00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:30.883 align:start size:69% position:31%
And so that was
out Saturday activity.

00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:33.286 align:start size:75% position:25%
Let's go to the library.
Let's go to the park.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:34.854
And that was kind of escaping

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:36.756
a lot of the segregation
in Crown Heights

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:39.926 size:81% position:59%
to go to this area where
the amenities were better.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:43.629 align:start position:0%
Did you notice those
neighborhoods being cleaner?

00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:46.365 align:start size:88% position:13%
Absolutely. And I think
the biggest thing was trees.

00:02:46.398 --> 00:02:48.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- Just like how many trees
  do you have on your block?

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.469 align:start size:75% position:25%
I think it does
something to your psyche
where it's like,

00:02:50.502 --> 00:02:52.271 align:start size:66% position:34%
oh, I feel like
I can breathe better.

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:55.341 align:start size:91% position:9%
I feel like someone cared
enough to beautify this area.

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:58.644 align:start position:0%
Well, guys, thank you
for taking the time out
to come look at this with me

00:02:58.677 --> 00:02:59.946 align:start size:81% position:19%
- and showing me
  where you grew up.
- This is so cool.

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.815 align:start position:0%
I want to talk
to this professor at NYU

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:08.421 align:start position:0%
to see if I can get some
information and some insight
into how we actually got here.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:12.325
Jacob:
<i> So I think the overarching
 important message to take away</i>

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:15.394 line:0%
is that segregation
was intentionally designed.

00:03:15.427 --> 00:03:16.929 align:start size:91% position:9%
- There are a number of--
- Designed by who?

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:20.299
By a wide range of policy makers
on the local level

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:21.634
all the way up
to the federal level.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.669 align:start size:66% position:34%
When did we start
to see the government

00:03:23.702 --> 00:03:26.939 align:start size:88% position:13%
actually leading
a lot of these programs
when it came to segregation?

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:28.975 align:start position:0%
So the federal government
instituted these

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.677
massive housing programs
during the New Deal

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.548
<i> to save a housing market
 that was in severe distress.</i>

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:39.518
<i> Their first policy
 was called the Home Owner's
 Loan Corporation,</i>

00:03:39.551 --> 00:03:41.520
<i> or H.O.L.C. or "hulk."</i>

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:44.991
<i> Part of H.O.L.C.
 was sending appraisers,</i>

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:48.527
<i> real estate appraisers
 to hundreds of cities
 across the country.</i>

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.398
<i> And these appraisers
 would grade neighborhoods
 in these cities,</i>

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.034
<i> and this is where the term
 redlining comes from.</i>

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.869
Lee:
<i> In your lease it would say,</i>

00:03:56.902 --> 00:03:58.771
<i> "You cannot resell this house
 to a black family."</i>

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:00.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Jacob:<i> Yeah. </i>
-<i> So, let me explain.</i>

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:04.377
As these appraisers surveyed
cities across the United States,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.779
they created maps that
they distributed to banks.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.447
And the banks used these maps

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.850
to decide how risky
it would be to provide loans

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:14.320
or mortgage assistance
to different neighborhoods
around the country,

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:17.757
and each of these maps
was assigned a color.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:21.594 align:start position:0%
So green indicated this
was the most desirable area,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:24.430
<i> and the map we're looking
 at right now is an area</i>

00:04:24.463 --> 00:04:26.432
<i> in the northern suburbs
 of Chicago.</i>

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:27.733 size:81% position:41%
And along with these maps,

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:30.469 align:start position:0%
we were given documents
that described

00:04:30.502 --> 00:04:32.405
<i> the neighborhood
 that was being surveyed.</i>

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:36.942 align:start position:0%
If we take a look at the top,
we can see the class
and occupation.

00:04:36.975 --> 00:04:39.312 align:start position:0%
We can also see the number
of black families in this area,

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.581 align:start position:0%
and the preferred nomenclature
of the 1930s was Negro.

00:04:42.614 --> 00:04:44.350 align:start position:0%
There were zero.

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:45.584 align:start size:72% position:28%
Moving on to
the next classification

00:04:45.617 --> 00:04:46.919 align:start size:75% position:25%
were blue neighborhoods.

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:48.421 align:start position:0%
And blue neighborhoods
were considered

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.022 align:start position:0%
to still be desirable.

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:51.757
So as we take a look
at the description

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.859
of this blue area in Chicago,

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:56.362 size:78% position:39%
the percentage of foreign
families is still zero.

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:58.931 align:start position:0%
The percentage
of Negro families, still none.

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:02.435 align:start position:0%
And as we come down
to the description, we see
the area is well laid out

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:03.903 align:start position:0%
and the improvements
are attractive,

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:07.573 align:start position:0%
but the proximity
of Negro families
on Spruce Street

00:05:07.606 --> 00:05:11.977
<i> at the southern edge
 precludes the district
 from a better rating</i>

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.413
<i> and is retarding
 its development.</i>

00:05:14.446 --> 00:05:18.851
The proximity
of Negro families.
That's so crazy.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:21.787
So as you can see,
these maps didn't ignore race.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.023
In fact, they were
heavily dependent on it.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:28.794 size:75% position:38%
Race played a key factor
in determining the value
of these neighborhoods

00:05:28.827 --> 00:05:31.564
and their ratings to the banks.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:35.634
The yellow neighborhoods were
listed as definitely declining.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.637
This one had
50% foreign families,

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.840 align:start position:0%
those foreign families
being Polish and German.

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.808 align:start position:0%
Race wasn't the only
determining factor

00:05:42.841 --> 00:05:44.977 align:start position:0%
in whether or not these
neighborhoods had value.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.980
Immigrant families heavily
impacted whether or not

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:50.416
it got a favorable evaluation.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:53.953 align:start size:72% position:28%
And as we move on
to the last designation
of these neighborhoods,

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:56.122
we come to red neighborhoods.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.492 align:start position:0%
And this is actually where
we get the term redlining from.

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.961 align:start position:0%
These neighborhoods
were defined as hazardous.

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.996
And when we look at
the description of the area,

00:06:04.029 --> 00:06:06.499
<i> the number of
 foreign families-- 5%.</i>

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:07.967
<i> They were Italians.</i>

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.070
But the percentage
of Negro families-- 90%.

00:06:11.103 --> 00:06:13.139 align:start position:0%
And as we make our way
down the description sheet,

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:17.042 align:start position:0%
we see "This concentration
of Negroes in Evanston

00:06:17.075 --> 00:06:19.178 align:start position:0%
is quite a serious problem
for the town,

00:06:19.211 --> 00:06:22.014
<i> as they seem to be
 growing steadily</i>

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.850
<i> and encroaching into
 adjoining neighborhoods."</i>

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:29.155
These maps are how
the federal government
institutionalized segregation,

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:32.958
and they used race
to determine the value of all
of these neighborhoods.

00:06:32.991 --> 00:06:36.529
So I want to understand
how all of these red lines

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:39.565
are impacting people's
lives today.

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:44.036 line:0%
H.O.L.C. was passed in 1933,
and before that time,

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:46.939 line:0%
places where H.O.L.C.
made those red-lining maps

00:06:46.972 --> 00:06:49.041 align:start size:69% position:31%
and places that didn't
have those maps

00:06:49.074 --> 00:06:51.677 align:start size:78% position:22%
were about the same level
of segregation.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:57.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the measurement
that we use is a fairly common
measurement of segregation

00:06:57.716 --> 00:06:59.885 align:start size:72% position:28%
called Black Isolation.

00:06:59.918 --> 00:07:04.156
Then by 1950, 1960,
we see that cities

00:07:04.189 --> 00:07:06.892
that were where
the redlining maps were created,

00:07:06.925 --> 00:07:09.094
there's this huge jump here.

00:07:09.127 --> 00:07:13.766 align:start position:0%
This one sort of moment,
they were collecting the data
and published the data

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:18.204 align:start position:0%
and it vastly impacted
millions of American lives
from that point on.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:23.776 size:78% position:61%
Yeah. This gap emerges
especially as the Federal
Housing Administration

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:26.912 align:start size:72% position:28%
and the G.I. Bill
kick in after H.O.L.C.,

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:30.583
and then we're layering on
schools, highways, et cetera.

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.586 align:start size:66% position:34%
Suburbanization.
And then all that
gets locked in place.

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.788 align:start size:78% position:22%
So I would say that
the gap that we see today

00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:39.959 align:start size:56% position:44%
is a result of
structural racism,

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:41.927
which includes the wealth gap,

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.598 align:start size:81% position:19%
it includes
this history of exclusion.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:50.236
It includes the intentional
segregating of neighborhoods.

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.938 align:start position:0%
What impact did this have
on our economy

00:07:52.971 --> 00:07:56.075 align:start position:0%
and the ability for people
to participate in it?

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:59.945 align:start size:63% position:38%
Housing segregation
has all these other
layers on top of it,

00:07:59.978 --> 00:08:03.816 size:78% position:61%
including education and
employment opportunities.

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:07.119 align:start size:59% position:41%
So the isolation
of people of color,

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:09.622 align:start size:78% position:22%
in this case specifically
African-Americans,

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:13.125 size:88% position:56%
has dramatically limited the
ability of African-Americans

00:08:13.158 --> 00:08:17.663 align:start size:91% position:9%
to kind of achieve what
we generally consider to be
kind of middle class status--

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:19.632 align:start size:53% position:47%
home ownership,
wealth stability,

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:25.070 align:start size:72% position:28%
the ability
to pass on resources
to the next generation.

00:08:25.103 --> 00:08:27.139 align:start position:0%
Any other interesting
takeaways from this?

00:08:27.172 --> 00:08:28.674
It didn't have to be this way.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.650 line:0%
So we just learned about how
the government has historically

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.985
segregated neighborhoods
across America,

00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:41.921
but I wanted to understand
how these problems

00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:43.589
are affecting people today.

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:46.158
I'm getting ready to meet up
with two former testers

00:08:46.191 --> 00:08:47.693
from the Fair Housing
Justice Center.

00:08:47.726 --> 00:08:50.996 align:start position:0% line:0%
So, tell me exactly
what a tester is.

00:08:51.029 --> 00:08:52.965 align:start position:0%
What did you all do
in that capacity?

00:08:52.998 --> 00:08:56.869 line:0%
It's a way of finding out what
landlords or real estate agents

00:08:56.902 --> 00:08:59.305 line:0%
are saying to people when they
think no one else is listening.

00:08:59.338 --> 00:09:03.776 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you're a real person
going out looking for a home
to rent or to buy,

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
you provide your information
and you're given
certain information

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:09.582 align:start size:69% position:31%
about what's available
in what neighborhoods
at what price levels.

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:14.186 align:start size:81% position:19%
You have no way if knowing
if someone of a different
race or ethnicity

00:09:14.219 --> 00:09:19.358 align:start size:91% position:9%
or any of the protected
characteristics is being told
the same thing as you.

00:09:19.391 --> 00:09:22.027 align:start position:0%
What are the ways that we're
seeing housing discrimination
play out right now?

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.330 align:start size:91% position:9%
Any time I hear somebody say,
"Oh, white supremacy,

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
white privilege is just a myth
in this country," I say,

00:09:27.032 --> 00:09:31.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Come do my job for half
an hour and you'll see the way
the doors get opened for me."

00:09:31.903 --> 00:09:34.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually had this
very nice woman in Brooklyn
showing me an apartment.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.676 align:start size:72% position:28%
We're always instructed
to convey disinterest.

00:09:37.709 --> 00:09:40.079 align:start size:88% position:13%
She said,
"Tell you what I'm gonna do.

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:44.049 align:start size:88% position:13%
Let me write down the number
for the entry code
for the lobby door.

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:48.220 align:start size:84% position:16%
If I'm not here,
you just let yourselves in.
Go up to the fourth floor.

00:09:48.253 --> 00:09:51.323 align:start size:91% position:9%
Show her around the apartment
because I'd really love
to see you guys in here."

00:09:51.356 --> 00:09:53.826
Most of my experiences
are not like Craig's at all.

00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:58.297 line:0%
I don't want to paint
the picture that every time
I'm sent out to test

00:09:58.330 --> 00:09:59.732 line:0%
I'm discriminated against,

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:02.368 align:start position:0% line:0%
but I have been
involved in cases

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.937 align:start position:0% line:0%
where very clear
discrimination happened.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:09.074 align:start size:91% position:9%
At times,
I am shown the apartment,
but I'm quoted a higher rent.

00:10:09.107 --> 00:10:12.244
$300 over my white counterpart.

00:10:12.277 --> 00:10:14.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
At times, not shown
amenities in the building.

00:10:14.413 --> 00:10:18.350
Not encouraged to apply
where my white counterpart
was encouraged to apply.

00:10:18.383 --> 00:10:23.722 align:start position:0%
Lisa, what is--
what do some of those
experiences that you've had,

00:10:23.755 --> 00:10:26.425 align:start position:0%
what do they tell you
about what regular folks
might be going through?

00:10:26.458 --> 00:10:30.663
When you're a tester, we're--
as an African-American,

00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:34.867
I'm representing all
the African-Americans that went
and inquired at that building

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:40.272
and were lied to,
so I represent so many
other people of color

00:10:40.305 --> 00:10:42.408
who are experiencing this
while just looking for housing,

00:10:42.441 --> 00:10:45.411
and housing is a basic need.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:50.949
So, when my dad
sold our house in D.C.,

00:10:50.982 --> 00:10:53.152
we had to get it
appraised twice.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:57.423
And the first time it was
appraised, the value was set
at about $800,000.

00:10:57.456 --> 00:10:59.992
But my dad knew that
our neighbors on the same block

00:11:00.025 --> 00:11:02.761
were selling their houses
for much more that that.

00:11:02.794 --> 00:11:04.430
So we spoke to a realtor
who told him

00:11:04.463 --> 00:11:07.132
to take all of
the family photos off the walls,

00:11:07.165 --> 00:11:10.436
leave them bare,
and get it appraised
a second time.

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:11.970
And that second appraisal,

00:11:12.003 --> 00:11:14.973
the value was set
at about $1.2 million.

00:11:15.006 --> 00:11:19.144
And at the time,
I didn't think that was
motivated by race,

00:11:19.177 --> 00:11:21.313
but now after talking
to these testers,

00:11:21.346 --> 00:11:23.082
<i> I'm starting
 to think that race</i>

00:11:23.115 --> 00:11:25.685
<i> maybe played a role
 in the home's valuation.</i>

00:11:27.819 --> 00:11:29.822 line:0%
- Toby?
- Yeah.

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.890 line:0%
- Hey, man, I'm Lee.
- Good to meet you.

00:11:31.923 --> 00:11:35.027 align:start position:0%
Tell a little bit
about what you guys do
at Open New York.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:36.462
So we are a pro-housing group.

00:11:36.495 --> 00:11:38.831 size:88% position:56%
We advocate for more housing

00:11:38.864 --> 00:11:42.000
and more affordable housing
all across New York.

00:11:42.033 --> 00:11:46.205 align:start position:0% line:0%
We are on 11th Street
between Fifth and Sixth Avenue
in Brooklyn Park Slope.

00:11:46.238 --> 00:11:49.041 line:0%
And we're looking at a building
that couldn't be built today

00:11:49.074 --> 00:11:52.211 align:start size:97% position:3%
- as of right now in Brooklyn.
- Why is that?

00:11:52.244 --> 00:11:54.346 align:start position:0%
So, the zoning rules
prohibit it.

00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:59.118 align:start position:0%
So, in 2003,
this area was downzoned.

00:11:59.151 --> 00:12:01.453
Lee: Why would people want
to downzone this neighborhood?

00:12:01.486 --> 00:12:04.857
If you owned here,
restricting supply
is in your interest

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.025
because it makes your
own property more valuable.

00:12:07.058 --> 00:12:12.030
And that's been the story
here for the last 40,
going on 50 years.

00:12:12.063 --> 00:12:15.501
Lee:<i> New York City
 famously rezoned nearly
 200,000 properties</i>

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:20.506
<i> between 2003 and 2007 under
 the Bloomberg administration.</i>

00:12:20.539 --> 00:12:23.509
<i> But a lot of this rezoning
 was actually downzoning.</i>

00:12:23.542 --> 00:12:29.047
<i> Here in Park Slope, some areas
 were downzoned to preserve
 the historic brownstones,</i>

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:33.452
<i> while other areas were rezoned
 to increase housing density.</i>

00:12:33.485 --> 00:12:38.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
How does this impact
segregation at a racial level,
at an economic level?

00:12:38.423 --> 00:12:39.892 align:start position:0%
It's brutal, right?

00:12:39.925 --> 00:12:41.527 size:81% position:41%
By privileging incumbents,

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.029 align:start position:0%
and by incumbents
I mean largely home owners,

00:12:44.062 --> 00:12:46.165 align:start position:0%
you're entrenching all
of your existing segregation.

00:12:46.198 --> 00:12:50.269 align:start position:0%
And let's be honest,
America is way
too segregated today.

00:12:50.302 --> 00:12:51.970 align:start size:63% position:38%
As so by downzoning,

00:12:52.003 --> 00:12:53.872 align:start size:66% position:34%
if this were
a white neighborhood,

00:12:53.905 --> 00:12:55.374 size:88% position:56%
you're making it much harder

00:12:55.407 --> 00:12:58.410 align:start size:63% position:38%
for it to become
a more diverse area.

00:12:58.443 --> 00:13:01.246 align:start position:0%
Absolutely.
You are actively
working against it.

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:05.918
( phone ringing )

00:13:05.951 --> 00:13:07.319 align:start size:59% position:41%
Fabiola, what's up?
How are you?

00:13:07.352 --> 00:13:09.254 align:start position:0%
Hey, Lee, I'm good.
How are you?

00:13:09.287 --> 00:13:12.090 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm good.
I just got in touch with
this mother in Philadelphia

00:13:12.123 --> 00:13:14.426 align:start size:91% position:9%
whose children are struggling
with lead poisoning.

00:13:14.459 --> 00:13:17.262
And I want to go talk to her
because I feel like this story

00:13:17.295 --> 00:13:21.967
might really embody
the very real consequences
of housing segregation.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.270
But I'm still looking
for someone to talk to
about home ownership

00:13:25.303 --> 00:13:28.373
and the racial wealth gap,
and I thought you might
have some ideas.

00:13:28.406 --> 00:13:32.110
Someone that
I've actually talked to before

00:13:32.143 --> 00:13:34.446
on this is Andre Perry.

00:13:34.479 --> 00:13:37.282
He's a Fellow at the Brookings
Institute, actually,

00:13:37.315 --> 00:13:40.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
- so he's based here in D.C.
- That's perfect.

00:13:40.318 --> 00:13:42.187 size:84% position:58%
Do you have time to talk
to him while I'm in Philly?

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:44.122
Fabiola:<i> Yeah. I'll see
if I can get in touch with him.</i>

00:13:44.155 --> 00:13:47.359 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Lee:<i> Amazing. </i>
- Why is home ownership

00:13:47.392 --> 00:13:50.128 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
important in terms
of economic mobility?

00:13:50.161 --> 00:13:52.130 align:start position:0% line:0%
Well, when you own a home,
you're more likely

00:13:52.163 --> 00:13:55.133 align:start position:0% line:0%
to be economically
and socially mobile.

00:13:55.166 --> 00:13:58.470 align:start position:0%
But home ownership also
precludes you from moving

00:13:58.503 --> 00:14:00.606 align:start position:0%
every time there's
an economic shock.

00:14:00.639 --> 00:14:02.975 align:start position:0%
Home ownership predicts
for better health,

00:14:03.008 --> 00:14:04.910 align:start position:0%
particularly
during a pandemic.

00:14:04.943 --> 00:14:08.313
I can't think of another asset

00:14:08.346 --> 00:14:11.350 align:start position:0%
that is so influential

00:14:11.383 --> 00:14:14.019 align:start position:0%
on a person's life chances
than housing.

00:14:14.052 --> 00:14:16.488 align:start size:66% position:34%
What have you found
about the differences
in property value

00:14:16.521 --> 00:14:18.490 align:start size:66% position:34%
between predominately
black neighborhoods

00:14:18.523 --> 00:14:19.658 align:start size:63% position:38%
and predominately
white neighborhoods?

00:14:19.691 --> 00:14:22.160
What does that chart tell us?

00:14:22.193 --> 00:14:24.162 line:0%
Andre:
Homes in neighborhoods
where the share

00:14:24.195 --> 00:14:27.332 line:0%
of the black population
is less than a percent

00:14:27.365 --> 00:14:31.436 line:0%
on average are priced
about $340,000.

00:14:31.469 --> 00:14:35.173 align:start position:0% line:0%
And as the percent
of black people

00:14:35.206 --> 00:14:36.642 line:0%
in a neighborhood increases,

00:14:36.675 --> 00:14:39.044 line:0%
you see
the home prices decrease.

00:14:39.077 --> 00:14:41.346 line:0%
So much so that when

00:14:41.379 --> 00:14:45.350 line:0%
the share of the black
population is 50% or higher,

00:14:45.383 --> 00:14:48.353 line:0%
the home prices on average
are about half as much

00:14:48.386 --> 00:14:50.222 line:0%
as their white neighborhoods.

00:14:50.255 --> 00:14:51.556 align:start size:53% position:47%
So you're saying
it's as simple as

00:14:51.589 --> 00:14:53.358 align:start size:66% position:34%
the more black people
in a neighborhood,

00:14:53.391 --> 00:14:55.460 align:start position:0%
- the less the houses are worth?
- That's exactly right.

00:14:55.493 --> 00:14:59.031 align:start position:0%
A lot of people will look
at that prior chart and say,

00:14:59.064 --> 00:15:02.200 align:start position:0%
"That's because of education.
That's because of crime."

00:15:02.233 --> 00:15:05.203 align:start position:0%
We control for education,
crime, walkability,

00:15:05.236 --> 00:15:06.672 line:0%
all those fancy Zillow metrics.

00:15:06.705 --> 00:15:09.341
And what we found is homes
in black neighborhoods

00:15:09.374 --> 00:15:12.544
are underpriced about 23%,

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:15.113
about $48,000 per home.

00:15:15.146 --> 00:15:19.251 align:start position:0%
Cumulatively,
that's about $156 billion
in lost equity.

00:15:19.284 --> 00:15:23.221
And how does racial segregation
factor into housing valuation?

00:15:23.254 --> 00:15:28.393 size:84% position:42%
Well, you know, segregation
has a long-standing impact

00:15:28.426 --> 00:15:30.228 align:start position:0%
on a number of things.

00:15:30.261 --> 00:15:34.299 align:start position:0%
Cities are becoming more
concentrated with poor folk,

00:15:34.332 --> 00:15:38.570 align:start position:0%
and that is reducing
those areas' abilities

00:15:38.603 --> 00:15:43.241 align:start position:0%
to pay for services
like education,

00:15:43.274 --> 00:15:45.577 size:78% position:39%
policing, infrastructure.

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:49.247 align:start position:0%
So when you concentrate
poverty in areas,

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:53.251 align:start position:0%
not only are you
putting a target on those
communities' back

00:15:53.284 --> 00:15:55.587
in terms of harmful policies,

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:59.391 align:start position:0%
but you have less resources
in a neighborhood.

00:15:59.424 --> 00:16:02.427
<i> We have got to address this
 because if folks</i>

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.730
<i> can't achieve
 the American dream
 through housing,</i>

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:08.100 size:81% position:41%
then they're not going to
achieve the American dream

00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:09.568 align:start position:0%
in any other part
of their life.

00:16:11.302 --> 00:16:13.271
So, I'm on my way
to Philadelphia,

00:16:13.304 --> 00:16:17.275
a city where
nearly 8% of the children
under the age of seven

00:16:17.308 --> 00:16:19.144
are struggling
with lead poisoning.

00:16:21.146 --> 00:16:23.615
Lead is a heavy metal,
and when it gets
into your blood,

00:16:23.648 --> 00:16:27.452
it can cause anything
from a reduced IQ to ADHD,

00:16:27.485 --> 00:16:32.290
and some research has actually
linked it to school failures
and even criminality.

00:16:32.323 --> 00:16:35.660
And while lead paint
has been illegal since 1978,

00:16:35.693 --> 00:16:39.631
a number of the homes
here in Philadelphia were built
well before then.

00:16:39.664 --> 00:16:41.299
So I'm getting ready
to meet up with a family

00:16:41.332 --> 00:16:45.604
whose lives have been reshaped
by their zip code.

00:16:48.473 --> 00:16:50.308 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Hey.
- Hi, how are you?

00:16:50.341 --> 00:16:52.144 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- I'm good. I'm Lee.
  Nice to meet you.
- I'm Angel.

00:16:52.177 --> 00:16:53.779 size:81% position:41% line:0%
So where are we right now?

00:16:53.812 --> 00:16:57.182 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
Oh, man, we're in the area
of Olney in Philadelphia.

00:16:57.215 --> 00:16:59.551 align:start position:0% line:0%
Okay. And you used to
live out here?

00:16:59.584 --> 00:17:03.789 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Yes, I did.
- So, your kids got sick
  living in this house?

00:17:03.822 --> 00:17:05.757 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Angel: Yes, they did.
- What happened?

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.159
Angel: I took them
to the doctor one day

00:17:07.192 --> 00:17:08.593
just for a random checkup,

00:17:08.626 --> 00:17:09.828 align:start size:69% position:31%
and I come to find out

00:17:09.861 --> 00:17:12.364 align:start size:69% position:31%
my daughter
had a nine lead level,

00:17:12.397 --> 00:17:15.333 align:start size:72% position:28%
and my son,
at the time he was two,

00:17:15.366 --> 00:17:17.402 align:start size:75% position:25%
he had an 18 lead level.

00:17:17.435 --> 00:17:19.604
Lee: Jesus.

00:17:19.637 --> 00:17:22.507 align:start position:0%
What happened
after you found out that your
children had lead poisoning?

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:24.409 align:start position:0%
- I contacted--
- Did you call the landlord or--

00:17:24.442 --> 00:17:25.844 align:start size:50% position:50%
Yes, I contacted
the landlord.

00:17:25.877 --> 00:17:29.314 align:start size:69% position:31%
The city were actually
called by the doctors.

00:17:29.347 --> 00:17:31.149 align:start size:78% position:22%
They had to do gun tests.

00:17:31.182 --> 00:17:32.818 align:start size:81% position:19%
My children's room,
which was the middle room,

00:17:32.851 --> 00:17:36.121
they had the highest lead levels
in the whole house.

00:17:36.154 --> 00:17:38.657 align:start position:0%
How were you dealing with this?
You know, you're working.
You're a working mom.

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:41.593 align:start size:91% position:9%
- You have three children.
- It's hard. It's hard.
  It's extremely hard.

00:17:41.626 --> 00:17:44.196 align:start size:59% position:41%
Who wants to have
their children
get lead poisoning?

00:17:44.229 --> 00:17:47.332 align:start size:75% position:25%
But when you are
a single parent,
you have three children,

00:17:47.365 --> 00:17:48.867 align:start size:72% position:28%
you're doing
everything by yourself,

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.237 align:start size:91% position:9%
how can you make enough money
to just up and leave?

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:55.307 align:start position:0%
What kind of a message
did that send you about,
you know, where you live?

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:59.211 align:start size:75% position:25%
You know, if you
were living center city,
it just wouldn't happen.

00:17:59.244 --> 00:18:01.113 align:start size:63% position:38%
The suburbs,
it would not happen.

00:18:01.146 --> 00:18:03.548
So when you made the decision
to get your kids out of here--

00:18:03.581 --> 00:18:05.717 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yes, I did.
I actually moved to a hotel.

00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:08.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
- For how long?
- For about a month,
  a month and a half.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Where are you living now?
- In Reading.

00:18:11.256 --> 00:18:14.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Reading? Where is that?
- Yes. About an hour and ten
  minutes away from here.

00:18:14.425 --> 00:18:15.694 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Can you show me where?
- Yeah.

00:18:20.331 --> 00:18:23.235 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- Lee: Okay, so this is
  your new house in Reading?
- Angel: Yes.

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:27.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So tell me about this place.
- Well, when I first got here,
  it looked immaculate.

00:18:27.438 --> 00:18:30.375 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right.
- Three days after,

00:18:30.408 --> 00:18:36.381 align:start position:0%
my walls started
to have a little crumbling
where I saw water.

00:18:36.414 --> 00:18:38.683 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Can you show me?
  Can we take a look?
- Yes. Yes.

00:18:38.716 --> 00:18:42.254
Up here, there's mold
all throughout the ceiling.

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:45.790
This is where the water
leaks at on the floor.

00:18:45.823 --> 00:18:49.427
And here, this is more
water damage.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.563 align:start position:0%
This right here was seeping
water the other day.

00:18:51.596 --> 00:18:54.900 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Jeez.
- There's mold down here

00:18:54.933 --> 00:18:57.602
just coming out the wall.

00:18:57.635 --> 00:19:00.639
So I went from one bad situation
to a worse situation.

00:19:00.672 --> 00:19:02.607
I'm literally on unemployment

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.276 align:start size:59% position:41%
because I was
laid off at my job.

00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:05.610 align:start size:66% position:34%
I have nowhere to go.

00:19:05.643 --> 00:19:09.281 align:start position:0%
When people say things like,
you know,

00:19:09.314 --> 00:19:10.749 align:start position:0%
America's the land
of opportunity.

00:19:10.782 --> 00:19:12.617 align:start position:0%
You just gotta work hard.
You just gotta pull yourself--

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:18.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's a lie.
Because I moved from
the last place to come here.

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:19.925 align:start size:63% position:38%
So what's next?

00:19:28.333 --> 00:19:31.269
Okay, so I know
that I grew up privileged.

00:19:31.302 --> 00:19:34.806
I know that I was
incredibly fortunate to have
attended great schools,

00:19:34.839 --> 00:19:38.643
to know from a young age
that I could afford
to pay for college

00:19:38.676 --> 00:19:40.812
<i> and to just have
 fresh air to breathe</i>

00:19:40.845 --> 00:19:43.815
<i> and clean water to drink.</i>

00:19:43.848 --> 00:19:45.483
<i> What I didn't know
 growing up</i>

00:19:45.516 --> 00:19:47.819
was that this was
an incredibly rare experience,

00:19:47.852 --> 00:19:50.255
especially if you're someone
that looks like me.

00:19:50.288 --> 00:19:53.225
<i> I had no idea that
 the full weight of history</i>

00:19:53.258 --> 00:19:57.329
<i>was actively working against me
 having that experience.</i>

00:19:57.362 --> 00:19:59.497
<i> My parents beat the odds,</i>

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:01.499
<i> largely because
 we always owned homes</i>

00:20:01.532 --> 00:20:03.835
<i> in this family
 going back generations,</i>

00:20:03.868 --> 00:20:07.305
<i> and that had a direct impact
 on my chances in life.</i>

00:20:07.338 --> 00:20:10.342
<i> But the scarcity of stories
 like mine isn't a problem</i>

00:20:10.375 --> 00:20:12.344
<i> that any one of us can fix.</i>

00:20:12.377 --> 00:20:16.648
It is, however,
something that we don't have
to passively accept.

00:20:16.681 --> 00:20:20.352
In trying to find out
whether or not your zip code
determines your future,

00:20:20.385 --> 00:20:22.621
I did see some bright spots.

00:20:22.654 --> 00:20:25.323
<i> For decades, zoning laws
 were used to restrict access.</i>

00:20:25.356 --> 00:20:29.427
<i> But now in some cities,
 they're being used
 to encourage investment</i>

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:31.963
<i> and create
 more diverse neighborhoods.</i>

00:20:31.996 --> 00:20:34.399
There are private firms
that are investing

00:20:34.432 --> 00:20:35.867
<i> in low income luxury housing</i>

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:38.937
that's meant to convert
renters into homeowners.

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.573
And some landlords and members
of the real estate industry

00:20:41.606 --> 00:20:44.542
<i> have woken up to the role
 that they've played</i>

00:20:44.575 --> 00:20:47.379
<i> in perpetuating
 housing segregation</i>

00:20:47.412 --> 00:20:51.716
<i> and restricting
 the upward mobility of our
 neighbors for generations.</i>

00:20:51.749 --> 00:20:56.888 align:start position:0%
For every dollar the typical
white household has,

00:20:56.921 --> 00:21:00.425 size:88% position:44%
the typical black household
has about nine or ten cents.

00:21:00.458 --> 00:21:05.397
And this is connected directly
to this legacy of redlining

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:10.435 align:start position:0%
and exclusion
from the opportunity
to build home equity.

00:21:10.468 --> 00:21:14.005
<i> So undoing that
 is going to require as big</i>

00:21:14.038 --> 00:21:16.374
<i> if not a bigger investment</i>

00:21:16.407 --> 00:21:19.044
than was made in the New Deal.

00:21:19.077 --> 00:21:20.912 size:75% position:38%
Let's excite the economy

00:21:20.945 --> 00:21:23.581 align:start position:0%
in the same way
we're doing during COVID,

00:21:23.614 --> 00:21:27.419 align:start position:0%
the same way we did after
the Great Depression,

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:29.054 align:start position:0%
but we just didn't do it
for black people.

00:21:29.087 --> 00:21:33.591 align:start position:0%
So now's the time to really
correct those wrongs.

00:21:33.624 --> 00:21:36.961
Ultimately, we can't reverse
the course of history

00:21:36.994 --> 00:21:39.431
without those
who dug this hole

